---
<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="45–55 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson 11</p>
  <h1>Days, months &amp; <em>dates</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Use a calendar in U.S. English. Name the seven <strong>days</strong> and twelve <strong>months</strong>, distinguish a weekday from a calendar date, and schedule an appointment with clear month–day order: <span class="ex">Tuesday, March 10</span>, spoken <span class="ex">Tuesday, March tenth</span>.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">45–55 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">on / in</span>
    <span class="tag">U.S. month–day order</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min optional practice</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Read a real calendar entry.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>When to use on and in.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Match, choose, and order.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Schedule your real week.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">Days and months always start with a <strong>capital letter</strong> in English: <span class="ex">Monday, Tuesday, January, March</span>. Seasons normally stay lowercase: <span class="ex">spring, summer, fall, winter</span>.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved the number word, the first letter of March, and a clarification request used before confirming calendar information.">
    <h3>Bring numbers and spelling into the calendar</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Complete each line from an earlier lesson, then say the full calendar exchange.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier lesson choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="thirteen">thirteen</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="thirty">thirty</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="M">M</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="N">N</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="repeat">repeat</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="spell">spell</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The appointment number is 13: number <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="thirteen" data-hint="13 ends in -teen; 30 ends in -ty." data-fix="The appointment number is thirteen." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(numbers)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>March begins with <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="M" data-hint="Say the first written letter in March." data-fix="March begins with M as in Mike." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> as in Mike. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(alphabet)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>You did not hear the date. Ask: “Could you <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="repeat" data-hint="Ask the speaker to say the same information again." data-fix="Could you repeat the date?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the date?” &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(clarification)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Read the calendar</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Meeting: on Monday &nbsp; · &nbsp; Deadline: on March 8th &nbsp; · &nbsp; Vacation: in July &nbsp; · &nbsp; New office: in 2026</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Two entries use <span class="ex">on</span> and two use <span class="ex">in</span>. Which words come <strong>after on</strong>? Which words come <strong>after in</strong>?</li>
      <li>One is an exact day. One is an exact date. One is a whole month. One is a whole year. Which is the <strong>biggest</strong> block of time?</li>
      <li>So which little word goes with an exact day or date, and which goes with a long block like a month or year?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <figure class="calendar" aria-label="March 2026 calendar with the 8th marked">
    <figcaption class="cal-head">March <span>2026</span></figcaption>
    <div class="cal-grid">
      <span class="cal-dow cal-we">Sun</span><span class="cal-dow">Mon</span><span class="cal-dow">Tue</span><span class="cal-dow">Wed</span><span class="cal-dow">Thu</span><span class="cal-dow">Fri</span><span class="cal-dow cal-we">Sat</span>
      <span class="cal-day">1</span><span class="cal-day">2</span><span class="cal-day">3</span><span class="cal-day">4</span><span class="cal-day">5</span><span class="cal-day">6</span><span class="cal-day">7</span>
      <span class="cal-day cal-mark">8</span><span class="cal-day">9</span><span class="cal-day">10</span><span class="cal-day">11</span><span class="cal-day">12</span><span class="cal-day">13</span><span class="cal-day">14</span>
      <span class="cal-day">15</span><span class="cal-day">16</span><span class="cal-day">17</span><span class="cal-day">18</span><span class="cal-day">19</span><span class="cal-day">20</span><span class="cal-day">21</span>
      <span class="cal-day">22</span><span class="cal-day">23</span><span class="cal-day">24</span><span class="cal-day">25</span><span class="cal-day">26</span><span class="cal-day">27</span><span class="cal-day">28</span>
      <span class="cal-day">29</span><span class="cal-day">30</span><span class="cal-day">31</span><span class="cal-day cal-empty"></span><span class="cal-day cal-empty"></span><span class="cal-day cal-empty"></span><span class="cal-day cal-empty"></span>
    </div>
  </figure>
  <p class="cal-cap">The whole month is <span class="ex">March</span> → <span class="ex">in March</span>. One exact date is <span class="ex">March 8</span> or informally <span class="ex">March 8th</span> (circled) → <span class="ex">on March 8th</span>. In speech, say <span class="ex">March eighth</span>.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. Exact days and dates take on; whole months and years take in.">
    <h3>Discovery check: on or in?</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use <b>on</b> for an exact day or date · use <b>in</b> for a month or a year</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="on">on</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="in">in</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The meeting is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on" data-hint="Tuesday names one exact day." data-fix="The meeting is on Tuesday." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Tuesday. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(a day)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>My birthday is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="in" data-hint="October names a whole month, not one exact date." data-fix="My birthday is in October." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> October. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(a month)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The deadline is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on" data-hint="March 8th identifies one exact calendar date." data-fix="The deadline is on March 8th." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> March 8th. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(a date)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>We moved here <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="in" data-hint="2026 names a whole year." data-fix="We moved here in 2026." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> 2026. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(a year)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The system: days, months, dates</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Learn the words as a set, then add the little words for time. Here is the full word bank for reference.</p>

  <div class="wordlist">
    <h3 class="wl-head">Days of the week</h3>
    <div class="wl-items"><span>Monday</span><span>Tuesday</span><span>Wednesday</span><span>Thursday</span><span>Friday</span><span>Saturday</span><span>Sunday</span></div>
    <p class="wl-note">A common workweek is <strong>Monday–Friday</strong>; <strong>Saturday</strong> and <strong>Sunday</strong> are the <strong>weekend</strong>. Many U.S. calendars display Sunday first, as the calendar above does. This lesson recites Monday first to keep the workweek and weekend together.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wordlist">
    <h3 class="wl-head">Months of the year</h3>
    <div class="wl-items"><span>January</span><span>February</span><span>March</span><span>April</span><span>May</span><span>June</span><span>July</span><span>August</span><span>September</span><span>October</span><span>November</span><span>December</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. Day asks for a weekday, date asks for a calendar date, and tomorrow or next Monday normally takes no preposition.">
    <h3>Meaning check: day, date, or no preposition?</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the expected answer to choose the exact question or time phrase.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Calendar meaning choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="day">day</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="date">date</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="tomorrow">tomorrow</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="on tomorrow">on tomorrow</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="next Monday">next Monday</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="on next Monday">on next Monday</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The answer is “Tuesday.” Ask: “What <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="day" data-hint="Tuesday names a weekday." data-fix="What day is it today? It's Tuesday." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> is it today?”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The answer is “March 10th.” Ask: “What's the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="date" data-hint="March 10th names a numbered calendar date." data-fix="What's the date today? It's March 10th." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> today?”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>We meet one day after today. Say: “See you <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="tomorrow" data-hint="Tomorrow already locates the meeting in time." data-fix="See you tomorrow." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>This week is busy. The call is on Monday of the following week. Say: “The call is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="next Monday" data-hint="Next before a weekday normally takes no on." data-fix="The call is next Monday." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="wordlist">
    <h3 class="wl-head">Seasons</h3>
    <div class="wl-items"><span>spring</span><span>summer</span><span>fall (also autumn)</span><span>winter</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="wordlist">
    <h3 class="wl-head">Key date words</h3>
    <div class="wl-items"><span>today</span><span>tomorrow</span><span>yesterday</span><span>week</span><span>month</span><span>year</span><span>weekend</span><span>birthday</span><span>date</span><span>calendar</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="wordlist">
    <h3 class="wl-head">Ordinal numbers (for dates)</h3>
    <div class="wl-items"><span>1st</span><span>2nd</span><span>3rd</span><span>4th</span><span>5th</span><span>6th</span><span>7th</span><span>8th</span><span>9th</span><span>10th</span><span>11th</span><span>12th</span><span>13th</span><span>14th</span><span>15th</span><span>16th</span><span>17th</span><span>18th</span><span>19th</span><span>20th</span><span>21st</span><span>22nd</span><span>23rd</span><span>24th</span><span>25th</span><span>26th</span><span>27th</span><span>28th</span><span>29th</span><span>30th</span><span>31st</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="wordlist">
    <h3 class="wl-head">Common phrases</h3>
    <ul class="wl-phrases">
      <li>“What day is it today?” → <span class="ex">It's Tuesday.</span></li>
      <li>“What's the date today?” → <span class="ex">It's March 10th.</span></li>
      <li>“My birthday is in <b>[month]</b>.”</li>
      <li>“See you on <b>[day]</b>.”</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>little word</th><th>use it for…</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>on</b></td><td>an exact day or date</td><td><span class="contraction">on Monday · on March 8th</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>in</b></td><td>a whole month or year</td><td><span class="contraction">in March · in 2026</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>no preposition</b></td><td>today, tomorrow, yesterday, this, next, or last</td><td><span class="contraction">today · next Monday · last year</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>U.S. calendar English: writing, speaking, and checking</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Common U.S. writing:</strong> month first, then the day: <span class="ex">March 8</span>. Informal writing also uses <span class="ex">March 8th</span>.</li>
      <li><strong>Speech:</strong> pronounce the day as an ordinal: <span class="ex">March eighth</span>, not <span class="ex">March eight</span>.</li>
      <li><strong>Add a year:</strong> write <span class="ex">March 8, 2026</span> with a comma. Say <span class="ex">March eighth, twenty twenty-six</span>.</li>
      <li><strong>Numeric order:</strong> in common U.S. use, <span class="ex">3/8/2026</span> means March 8, 2026. In other countries, the same numbers may mean August 3. For international or important information, write the month name and read the date back.</li>
      <li><strong>Day or date?</strong> <span class="ex">What day is it today?</span> asks for a weekday. <span class="ex">What's the date today?</span> asks for a calendar date.</li>
      <li><strong>No extra preposition:</strong> say <span class="ex">See you tomorrow</span> and <span class="ex">See you next Monday</span>, not <span class="ex">on tomorrow</span> or <span class="ex">on next Monday</span>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Ordinal endings for the days of a month</h3>
    <p>The ending follows the spoken ordinal. Most dates use <span class="ex">-th</span>, but remember the main families:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="ex">1st first · 2nd second · 3rd third</span></li>
      <li><span class="ex">4th fourth · 5th fifth · 8th eighth · 9th ninth · 12th twelfth</span></li>
      <li><span class="ex">11th eleventh · 12th twelfth · 13th thirteenth</span> are exceptions to the final digit pattern.</li>
      <li>The pattern returns in <span class="ex">21st twenty-first · 22nd twenty-second · 23rd twenty-third · 31st thirty-first</span>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: hear the strong part of the calendar word</h3>
    <p>Most day names begin with a clear beat and finish with a light <span class="ex">day</span>. Some month and date words do not sound exactly the way every letter looks.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>word</th><th>say it in parts</th><th>what to notice</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Monday · Tuesday</td><td><span class="ex"><strong>MON</strong>-day · <strong>TOOZ</strong>-day</span></td><td>in common U.S. speech, the first part is stronger</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wednesday</td><td><span class="ex"><strong>WENZ</strong>-day</span></td><td>it has two spoken parts; the middle letters are not heard separately</td></tr>
        <tr><td>July · September</td><td><span class="ex">ju-<strong>LY</strong> · sep-<strong>TEM</strong>-ber</span></td><td>the strong beat is later in the word</td></tr>
        <tr><td>13th · 30th</td><td><span class="ex">thir-<strong>TEENTH</strong> · <strong>THIR</strong>-ti-eth</span></td><td>the stress helps the listener hear the correct date</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>For endings such as <span class="ex">fourth</span>, <span class="ex">eighth</span>, and <span class="ex">tenth</span>, place the tongue gently between the teeth and let the air out for <span class="ex">th</span>. Say the full date as one chunk: <span class="ex"><strong>MARCH EIGHTH</strong></span>.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read day and date listening check">
    <h2>Hear the calendar information</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Keep the script closed while you listen. Your teacher reads each line once, then once more. Choose the exact day, month, or date you hear.</p>

    <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated similar day names, month stress, and ordinal date forms in six short schedule messages.">
      <h3>Listening check: complete the schedule</h3>
      <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Listening calendar choices">
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Tuesday">Tuesday</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Thursday">Thursday</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Wednesday">Wednesday</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Friday">Friday</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="June">June</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="July">July</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="13th">13th</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="30th">30th</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="May 5th">May 5th</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="May 15th">May 15th</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="March 8th">March 8th</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="August 3rd">August 3rd</button>
      </div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The appointment is on <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Tuesday" data-hint="Listen for the common U.S. opening sound “TOOZ.”" data-fix="The appointment is on Tuesday." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The class is on <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Wednesday" data-hint="Listen for two spoken parts: WENZ-day." data-fix="The class is on Wednesday." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The vacation begins in <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="July" data-hint="Listen for the strong second beat: ju-LY." data-fix="The vacation begins in July." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The deadline is on March <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="13th" data-hint="Listen for the strong -teenth ending rather than first-syllable stress in thirtieth." data-fix="The deadline is on March 13th, spoken March thirteenth." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The call is on <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="May 15th" data-hint="Listen for fifteenth, not fifth." data-fix="The call is on May 15th, spoken May fifteenth." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The U.S. date is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="March 8th" data-hint="Listen for March first and eighth second." data-fix="The U.S. date is March 8th, spoken March eighth." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="drill-actions">
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
      </div>
      <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    </div>

    <details class="note">
      <summary>Tutor script · open only after the learner answers</summary>
      <p>Read naturally without showing the words: <span class="ex">1. The appointment is on Tuesday. 2. The class is on Wednesday. 3. The vacation begins in July. 4. The deadline is on March thirteenth. 5. The call is on May fifteenth. 6. The U.S. date is March eighth.</span></p>
      <p>For a second round, change one item and ask the learner to correct the written schedule.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <h2>Match the calendar words</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>

  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-game data-success-message="Correct. Monday and Friday mark the common U.S. workweek, while January and December mark the first and last months.">
    <h3>Drill 1: match each word to its place on the calendar</h3>
    <p class="tile-target">Tap a word, then tap the fact that fits it</p>
    <div class="tile-bank">
      <button class="tile" data-tile="monday">Monday</button>
      <button class="tile" data-tile="friday">Friday</button>
      <button class="tile" data-tile="january">January</button>
      <button class="tile" data-tile="december">December</button>
    </div>
    <div class="slot-board">
      <button class="slot" data-slot="monday" data-hint="A common U.S. workweek begins after Sunday." data-fix="Monday → first day of the common workweek" data-placeholder="first day of a common workweek">first day of a common workweek</button>
      <button class="slot" data-slot="friday" data-hint="A common U.S. workweek ends before Saturday." data-fix="Friday → last day of the common workweek" data-placeholder="last day of a common workweek">last day of a common workweek</button>
      <button class="slot" data-slot="january" data-hint="This month starts a new calendar year." data-fix="January → month 1" data-placeholder="month 1 of the year">month 1 of the year</button>
      <button class="slot" data-slot="december" data-hint="This month ends the calendar year." data-fix="December → month 12" data-placeholder="month 12 of the year">month 12 of the year</button>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-tiles>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-tiles>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose the right word</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You placed Wednesday and Saturday in the week and February and July in the year.">
    <h3>Drill 2: read the cue, choose the day or month</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">The hint in brackets tells you exactly which word fits</p>
